        conceptEn: "In TypeScript you can write function f<const T extends readonly unknown[]>(xs: T): T so inference keeps a literal tuple, as if the caller had written as const. Without const T, f(['a', 'b']) often infers string[]. With const T you get readonly ['a', 'b'], and (typeof result)[number] is then 'a' | 'b'. That is handy for route factories, CSS classes and dictionaries. A const type parameter does not make the argument immutable at runtime, it only asks for narrower inference. It can be combined with extends string.",
